{"answer":"Read, Glob, and Grep are repository-scoped: each resolves its user-supplied path through `resolve_in_root`; Grep also runs `rg` with the repository as its working directory. Read rejects files over 10 MiB and caps output at 2,000 lines/2,000 characters per line/32 KiB; Glob and Grep cap results at 100 entries/lines and 32 KiB, returning continuation guidance when truncated. Glob additionally excludes common generated/dependency directories and sorts matches deterministically (newest first, then path).

`RepoTools::call_many` sends calls to `execute_tools`, which maintains at most the configured number in flight (default 8), starts additional calls only as slots free, and wraps every call in a per-tool timeout (default 10 seconds). Completed futures may arrive in any order, but each result is stored by its input index and collected in input order; timeouts become failed `ToolResult`s with a deterministic timeout reminder.

Regression tests cover concurrent execution and input-order preservation, plus bounded Glob and Grep output with continuation notices. The repository has no dedicated timeout test in the inspected tool-test module; timeout behavior is implemented directly in the executor.","citations":[{"end_line":91,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/glob_tool.rs","reason":"Glob resolves the directory inside the repository and executes filesystem traversal in a blocking task." ,"start_line":59},{"end_line":220,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/grep.rs","reason":"Grep resolves the search path, constrains rg to the repository, and applies bounded output handling." ,"start_line":118},{"end_line":71,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/exec.rs","reason":"Defines concurrency and timeout defaults, bounded in-flight scheduling, timeout conversion, and index-based deterministic result collection." ,"start_line":7},{"end_line":127,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/exec.rs","reason":"Regression test verifies parallel execution and preservation of call order; Glob/Grep tests separately verify output bounds and continuation guidance." ,"start_line":77}]}

{"answer":"`RepoTools` constructs Read, Glob, and Grep with one repository root and dispatches calls only to those tools. Each tool resolves its requested path/directory through `resolve_in_root`; Grep also runs `rg` with the repository as its working directory and explicitly prevents glob traversal outside it. Read rejects oversized files, while Glob caps returned matches with `DEFAULT_LIMIT`.

`call_many` delegates to `execute_tools`: it maintains a bounded in-flight set (`concurrency`, default 8), starts calls concurrently, wraps every call in `tokio::time::timeout`, and returns a synthetic failed result on expiry. Although completions arrive through `FuturesUnordered`, each result is stored by its original index and collected in input order, making output deterministic.

The regression test verifies genuine overlap (four 500-ms calls complete under 1.2 s with peak concurrency ≥2) and verifies returned IDs retain input order.","citations":[{"end_line":107,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/lib.rs","reason":"Shows repository-root construction, Read/Glob/Grep dispatch, and call_many delegation." ,"start_line":38},{"end_line":145,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/read.rs","reason":"Shows Read path sandboxing and file-size bound." ,"start_line":103},{"end_line":180,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/glob_tool.rs","reason":"Shows Glob directory sandboxing, repository walk, deterministic sorting, and result limit." ,"start_line":71},{"end_line":63,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/exec.rs","reason":"Shows bounded concurrency, per-call timeout handling, indexed result storage, and deterministic collection." ,"start_line":15},{"end_line":128,"path":"crates/repo-tools/src/exec.rs","reason":"Regression test verifies concurrent execution, bounded elapsed time, and input-order results." ,"start_line":93}]}

{"answer":"Read, Glob, and Grep are sandboxed by resolving user paths through the repository root; Read and Glob reject escapes, while Grep runs `rg` from the root and passes only the root-relative search path. Read additionally rejects non-files, oversized files, and binary data, and caps returned lines. Glob ignores common generated/dependency directories, sorts matches newest-first with path tie-breaking, and applies a fixed result limit. Grep applies fixed ignore globs and supports caller-provided `head_limit` bounds.

Calls execute concurrently through `FuturesUnordered`, with at most the configured concurrency (default 8). Each call is wrapped in a per-tool timeout (default 10 seconds); timed-out calls become failed `ToolResult`s. Completion order does not affect output order: each result is stored under its input index and collected in original call order.

